\textbf{\textcolor{red}{AIMS OF TIME SERIES ANALYSIS}}

$\to$ \textbf{Describe} -- trend, seasonal, cycle (Often, to de-trend, de-seasonalize). If multivariate: find common patterns, clusters, dim. reductions etc... 

$\to$ \textbf{Explain} 
- modeling and inference write a model and estimate its parameters
- \textbf{Forecast} accounting for uncertainty

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{TIME SERIES DECOMPOSITION}}

\textbf{0.} 
View $Y_t$ as composed of: a \textbf{Trend}, a \textbf{Seasonal} comp., a \textbf{cycle}, and an \textbf{Erratic} comp.

\textbf{Additive}: $Y_t = T_t + S_t + C_t + E_t$

\textbf{Multiplicative}: $Y_t = T_t \cdot S_t \cdot C_t \cdot E_t$

note $\log$ transforms a multiplicative decomposition into an additive one

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FITTING A TREND}}

For $Y_t = T_t + E_t$ (no seasonal comp.):

\textbf{1. OLS.} 
\textbf{2. Moving averages.} e.g.\ MA of order $k=3$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1}}{3}, t = 2, 3, \dots \]
If $k$ is even, e.g.\ $k = 4$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{0.5\, y_{t-2} + y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1} + 0.5\, y_{t+2}}{4}. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{DETRENDING}}
\[ y_t^{\text{detrended}} = y_t - \widehat{T}_t. \]

after you detrend, you remove the seasonal coponent

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{HOW TO ASSIGN THE PROBABILITY LAW OF THE PROCESS}}



\begin{keydef}

\textbf{8.a} Defining a \textbf{statistical model} for a stoch.\ process $(Y_t)_{t \in T}$ requires specifying its probabilty law $\mathbb{P}$ first.  

By Kolmogorov's extension theorem, provided some \emph{consistency conditions},  $\mathbb{P}$ can be uniquely identified by the family of \textbf{finite-dimensional distributions (FDDs)}
\[
\Bigl\{
\underbrace{f_{Y_{t_1},\dots,Y_{t_k}}}_{\text{Joint distribution}}
\bigl(
\underbrace{y_1,\dots,y_k}_{\text{evaluated at}}
\bigr)
\;\Big|\;
k\ge1,\ 
(\underbrace{t_1,\dots,t_k}_{\text{at }k\text{ times}})
\in \underbrace{T^k}_{\text{Tuples in T}}
\Bigr\}
\]
\end{keydef}
Basically, you want to know
- The margianl at every t eg $Y_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_t, \sigma_t^2)$ for each $t$. 
- The bivariate densities $p_{s,t}(y_s, y_t)$ capturing dependences eg $\mathrm{Cov}(Y_s, Y_t) = 0$ for $s \neq t$ gives an iid process (under some assumptions); note from the marginals you cannot get the bivariates! (if you do not know how what is the correlation structure) 


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{MOST TRIVIAL EXAMPLE OF STOCH.\ PROCESS}}

\textbf{8.b IID stochastic processes}
Say $Y_t,\ t \ge 0$ are independent. If, additionally, $Y_t$ have a common distribution (i.i.d.) with $p$:
\[ p(y_{0:T}) \;=\; p(y_0)\, p(y_1) \cdots p(y_T) \;=\; \prod_{t=0}^{T} p(y_t). \]

\emph{Example} Independent trials $Y_t \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim}$ Bernoulli$(p)$.

Transition matrix:
\[ \begin{array}{c|cc} t-1 \backslash t & 0 & 1 \\ \hline 0 & 1-p & p \\ 1 & 1-p & p \end{array} \]


\textbf{8.c Independent (not necessarily identically distributed) stochastic processes}
If only indep.\ (not necessarily identically distrib.):
\[ p(y_{0:T}) \;=\; p_0(y_0)\, p_1(y_1) \cdots p_T(y_T) \;=\; \prod_{t=0}^{T} p_t(y_t). \]



Both assume independence: irrealistic for time series

\textbf{8.d Full dependence (general chain rule).}
\[ p(y_{0:T}) \;=\; p_0(y_0)\, p_1(y_1 \mid y_0)\, p_2(y_2 \mid y_0, y_1) \cdots p_T(y_T \mid y_{0:T-1}) \;=\; p_0(y_0) \prod_{t=1}^{T} p_t(y_t \mid y_{0:t-1}). \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{SUMMARIES OF A STOCHASTIC PROCESS}}

\textbf{3. Summaries:} 

$\bullet$ \textbf{Mean function}  $\mu : t \mapsto \mu(t) = \mathbb{E}[Y_t],  t = 1, 2, \dots$

$\bullet$ \textbf{Variance function}  $\sigma^2 : t \mapsto \sigma^2(t) = \mathrm{Var}(Y_t),  t = 1, 2, \dots$

$\bullet$ \textbf{Autocovariance function}  $\gamma : (t, t') \mapsto \gamma(t, t') = \mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t'}), $\quad$  t, t' = 1, 2, \dots$

$\bullet$ \textbf{Autocorrelation function}  $\rho : (t, t') \mapsto \rho(t, t') = \dfrac{\gamma(t, t')}{\sqrt{\sigma^2(t)\, \sigma^2(t')}}, $\quad$  t, t' = 1, 2, \dots$

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{STATIONARITY}}

\begin{keydef}
5.A time series $(Y_t)$ is \textbf{stationary} if $E(Y_t)$ and $E(Y_t Y_{t+h})$ exist, are finite, and do not depend on $t$, for every $h = 0, 1, 2, \dots$
\[ \mu(t) = \mu, \qquad \sigma^2(t) = \sigma^2, \qquad \gamma(t, t+h) = \tilde\gamma(h) \quad \text{(autocov.}. \]
\end{keydef}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{STRICT STATIONARITY}}

\begin{keydef}
4. A time series $(Y_t)_{t \in T}$ is \textbf{strictly stationary} if the joint distribution of $(Y_{t_1}, \dots, Y_{t_k}) \stackrel{d}{=} (Y_{t_1+h}, \dots, Y_{t_k+h}), \qquad \text{for any } (t_1, \dots, t_k) \text{ and } h $
\end{keydef}

Obviously this implies 
- k = 1 : every $Y_t$ has the same marginal
- k = 2: every bivariate depens on lag only, ot time. 
- weak stationarity

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{EXAMPLE 2 — CATEGORICAL TIME SERIES (PANEL ON VOTE INTENTION)}}

**14** Only the things in yellow! 
Usual assumptions

\textbf{Transition matrix} (given as data for the exercises):

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
$t-1 \;\backslash\; t$ & $1$ (Rep.) & $2$ (Dem.) & $3$ (Don't know) \\
\hline
$1$ (Rep.)        & $0.6$ & $0.3$ & $0.1$ \\
$2$ (Dem.)        & $0.1$ & $0.7$ & $0.2$ \\
$3$ (Don't know)  & $0.0$ & $0.4$ & $0.6$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\textit{Initial state:} at $t = 0$, the person is undecided, $Y_0 = 3$. Note we have no p0 here! (better, it is deterministic!)

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Worked exercises}}


\textbf{(b) Probability that at $t = 1$ she is still undecided and at $t = 2$ she expresses a preference for the Democrat Party.}\[ P(Y_1 = 3,\ Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_0 = 3) = P(Y_1 = 3 \mid Y_0 = 3)\, P(Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_1 = 3) = p_{3,3}\, p_{3,2} = 0.6 \times 0.4 = 0.24. \]

\textbf{(c) Probability that she expresses a preference for the Democrat Party at $t = 2$.}\[ P(Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_0 = 3) = \sum_{k=1}^{3} P(Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_1 = k)\, P(Y_1 = k \mid Y_0 = 3) = \sum_{k=1}^{3} p_{k,2}\, p_{3,k} = 0.52. \]
\textbf{(d) Given that she expresses a preference for the Democrat Party at $t = 2$, probability that she was still undecided at $t = 1$.}

Recall conditional probability (+ 1 extra conditioning layer):
\begin{keydef}
 \[ P(A \mid B,\, C) = \frac{P(A \cap B \mid C)}{P(B \mid C)}. \]
\end{keydef}

\emph{In our case:} take $A = \{Y_1 = 3\}$, $B = \{Y_2 = 2\}$, $C = \{Y_0 = 3\}$. Then\[ P\bigl(\underbrace{Y_1 = 3}_{A} \,\big|\, \underbrace{Y_2 = 2}_{B},\ \underbrace{Y_0 = 3}_{C}\bigr) = \frac{P\bigl(\underbrace{Y_1 = 3,\ Y_2 = 2}_{A \cap B} \mid \underbrace{Y_0 = 3}_{C}\bigr)}{P\bigl(\underbrace{Y_2 = 2}_{B} \mid \underbrace{Y_0 = 3}_{C}\bigr)}. \]

The numerator is exactly exercise (b) ($= 0.24$) and the denominator is exactly exercise (c) \]

\textbf{(e1, e2) Full distribution of $Y_2$ and $Y_3$, given $Y_0 = 3$ --- unified view.}

\emph{One-step recursion.} For any homogeneous Markov chain, the marginal distribution propagates by right-multiplication by $\mathbf{P}$:
\begin{keydef}
\[ \boldsymbol\pi_{n+1} \;=\; \boldsymbol\pi_n\, \mathbf{P} \]
\end{keydef}

Both (e1) and (e2) are instances at consecutive steps. Initial datum: $Y_0 = 3$ deterministically.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(e1) Distribution of $Y_2$.}} 
In matrix-vector form $\boldsymbol\pi_2 = \boldsymbol\pi_1\, \mathbf{P}$. As you started from  $Y_0 = 3$, you have that $\pi_1$ = $(0,\, 0.4,\, 0.6)$
\[ \boldsymbol\pi_2 \;=\; (0,\, 0.4,\, 0.6) \begin{pmatrix} 0.6 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\ 0.1 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\ 0.0 & 0.4 & 0.6 \end{pmatrix} \;=\; (\underbrace{0.04}_{P(Y_2 = 1 \mid Y_0 = 3)},\, \underbrace{0.52}_{P(Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_0 = 3)},\, \underbrace{0.44}_{P(Y_2 = 3 \mid Y_0 = 3)}). \]
If this makes you feel better...
$\bullet$ $P(Y_2 = 1 \mid Y_0 = 3) = p_{1,1}\, p_{3,1} + p_{2,1}\, p_{3,2} + p_{3,1}\, p_{3,3} = 0.6 \cdot 0 + 0.1 \cdot 0.4 + 0 \cdot 0.6 = 0.04$.

$\bullet$ $P(Y_2 = 2 \mid Y_0 = 3) = p_{1,2}\, p_{3,1} + p_{2,2}\, p_{3,2} + p_{3,2}\, p_{3,3} = 0.3 \cdot 0 + 0.7 \cdot 0.4 + 0.4 \cdot 0.6 = 0.52$ (matches (c)).

$\bullet$ $P(Y_2 = 3 \mid Y_0 = 3) = p_{1,3}\, p_{3,1} + p_{2,3}\, p_{3,2} + p_{3,3}\, p_{3,3} = 0.1 \cdot 0 + 0.2 \cdot 0.4 + 0.6 \cdot 0.6 = 0.44$.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(e2) Distribution of $Y_3$.}} 

In matrix-vector form $\boldsymbol\pi_3 = \boldsymbol\pi_2\, \mathbf{P}$:
\[ \boldsymbol\pi_3 \;=\; (0.04,\, 0.52,\, 0.44) \begin{pmatrix} 0.6 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\ 0.1 & 0.7 & 0.2 \\ 0.0 & 0.4 & 0.6 \end{pmatrix} \;=\; (0.076,\, 0.552,\, 0.372). \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{CONDITIONS OF THEOREM 2.1 — RECURRENT, APERIODIC, IRREDUCIBLE}}

\textbf{Recurrence / transience.}
\begin{keydef}
**11a** Consider the Markov chain started at $Y_0 = j$. Let
\[ f_j \;=\; P(\text{the chain returns at least once to } j \mid Y_0 = j). \]

$\bullet$ \textbf{Recurrent}: $f_j = 1$
$\bullet$ \textbf{Transient}: $f_j < 1$
$\bullet$ \textbf{Recurrent non-null}: $f_j = 1$ \emph{and} the expected return time is finite. This is what Theorem 2.1 needs.
\end{keydef}

\textbf{Periodicity.} 
\begin{keydef}
**11b**  State $j$ has \textbf{period $d$} if a return to $j$ is possible only at multiples of $d$ steps 
\end{keydef}

\textbf{Closed set / irreducibility.}
\begin{keydef}
**11c** A set $C \subseteq \mathcal{Y}$ is \textbf{closed} if: $p_{j,k} = 0 \quad \text{whenever } j \in C \text{ and } k \notin C.$ 
\textbf{Irreducible Markov Chain} if there are no closed sets $\rightarrow $ equivalently, every state is reachable from every other state in finitely many steps.
\end{keydef}
\emph{Example (reducible chain).} On $\mathcal{Y} = \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$,
\[ \mathbf{P} = \begin{pmatrix} 1/3 & 0 & 2/3 & 0 \\ 0 & 1/4 & 0 & 3/4 \\ 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 & 0 \\ 0 & 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 \end{pmatrix} \]
is reducible: the two sets $C_1 = \{1, 3\}$ and $C_2 = \{2, 4\}$ are each closed (no row in $C_1$ has positive mass on a column in $C_2$, and vice versa). The chain has no common limit distribution --- the long-run behaviour depends on which basin you start in.

\textbf{Consequences for an irreducible chain.}
\begin{keydef}
**11d** For an irreducible state: 
$\bullet$ \emph{Class property.} All states share the same recurrence status: either all recurrent, or all transient. 
$\bullet$ \emph{Finite state space.} If $|\mathcal{Y}| < \infty$, at least one state is visited infinitely often = recurrent. By the class property, \emph{all} states are positive recurrent. This guarantees the strict positivity $p^*(k) > 0$ in Theorem 2.1.
$\bullet$ \emph{Aperiodicity is still needed.} Recurrent + irreducible alone is not enough --- a periodic chain (e.g.\ alternating between two states) doesn't converge to a stationary marginal; it oscillates. Theorem 2.1 adds aperiodicity to rule this out.
\end{keydef}

\begin{keydef}
\textbf{1. A time series} is a stochastic process $(Y_t)_{t \in T }$ with $Y_t \in Y$. The data $(y1, . . . , yt)$ are he initial trait of (<- finite realization) of the stochastic process. 
\end{keydef} 


\begin{keydef}
\textbf{2. A stochastic process} $(Y_t)_{t \in T}$ is a sequence of random vectors (a multidimensional random variable) indexed by $t \in T$. 
\end{keydef} 

Note:
- This is not a random variable, nor a random vector. It is a sequence of random variables\vectors! So eg the p. of a repeated game.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{INFERENCE FOR MARKOV CHAINS}} \emph{(panel of i.i.d.\ chains)}

\begin{keydef}
**12** 
0. \textbf{Setting}: $n$ trajectories (chains) observed T times: $(Y_{k,0}, \dots, Y_{k,T})$ for $k = 1, \dots, n$, $h = 1, 2... M$ states. 

1. \textbf{Modelling assumptions:}  M1) each chain is homogeneous Markov, M2) individuals k are independent = chains are independent


2. \textbf{Find the MLE.}

\[ \underbrace{L(\mathbf{P};\, y_{1:n,1:T} | y_{1:n,0} ) }_{\text{For every of the n individuals a chain long T}} \;\overset{(1)}{=}\; \underbrace{\prod_{k=1}^{n} P\bigl(Y_{k,1:T} \mid \underbrace{y_{k,0}}_{\text{init value for indiv k}}, \mathbf{P}\bigr)}_{(1)\ \ \text{iid across each indiv }k} \;\overset{(2)}{=}\; \underbrace{\prod_{k=1}^{n} \prod_{t=1}^{T} P\bigl(Y_{k,t} \mid y_{k,0:t-1}, \mathbf{P}\bigr)}_{(2)\ \text{expand $Y_{1:T}$ + chain rule (you've already seen this Product in T)}} \;\overset{(3)}{=}\; \underbrace{\prod_{k=1}^{n} \prod_{t=1}^{T} P\bigl(Y_{k,t} \mid y_{k,t-1}, \mathbf{P}\bigr)}_{(3)\ (M1)\ \text{Markov drops deep history}} \;\overset{(4)}{=}\; \underbrace{\prod_{k=1}^{n} \prod_{t=1}^{T} p_{y_{k,t-1},\, y_{k,t}}}_{\text{notation}}. \]   
note if you do not condition by $y_{1:n,0}$ you have $L(\mathbf{P} , p_0 ; \, y_{1:n,0:T})$   

Now regroup by transition counts: \[ \prod_{i=1}^{M}\prod_{j=1}^{M} \underbrace{p_{i,j}^{\, n_{i,j}}}_{g(T(x), \theta))}. \] So the statistic $T(x) = \{n_{i,j}\}$ is \textbf{jointly sufficient} for $\mathbf{P}$ . *See note about the exponent. 

\emph{Step 3:} find MLE by maximise L wrt $p_{i,j}$ and you get: \[ \boxed{\hat p_{i,j} \;=\; \frac{n_{i,j}}{n_{i,+}}, \qquad i, j = 1, \dots, M.} \]
\end{keydef}


\textit{Caveats one may want to relax:} higher-order Markov (memory $r > 1$); non-homogeneity ($\mathbf{P}_t$ depends on $t$); individual-specific transition matrices (heterogeneity).


*Coin flip, outtcome: H, H, T, H. Probability of this exact sequence:$$p \cdot p \cdot (1-p) \cdot p ;=; p^{,3} \cdot (1-p)^{,1}.$$ In short:  $$\text{exponent on } p_{i,j} = \text{how many times you observed the transition } i \to j.$$

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{SUPERPOSITION PRINCIPLE --- additive component DLMs (Petris \S3.2).}}

\emph{Idea.} Build a complex DLM by \emph{adding together} $k$ simpler, mutually independent component DLMs that share one observation. Modular: pick trend + seasonal + AR + regression as bricks, stack their states, and you have a single combined DLM that the Kalman filter handles in one pass.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Setup --- $k$ independent component DLMs.}}

For each $j = 1, \dots, k$, component $j$ is its own full DLM (state $+$ obs noise) with state $\theta_{j,t} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_j}$:
\[ \theta_{j,t} = G_j\, \theta_{j,t-1} + w_{j,t}, \quad w_{j,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, W_j), \qquad Y_{j,t} = F_j\, \theta_{j,t} + v_{j,t}, \quad v_{j,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, V_j). \]
All shocks independent across components AND across time: $w_{j,t} \perp\!\!\!\perp w_{l,t}$, $v_{j,t} \perp\!\!\!\perp v_{l,t}$ for $j \ne l$, and $\theta_{j,0}$'s independent.

\emph{Observation as a sum.}
\[ Y_t \;=\; Y_{1,t} + Y_{2,t} + \cdots + Y_{k,t} \;=\; \sum_{j=1}^k F_j\, \theta_{j,t} \;+\; \underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^k v_{j,t}}_{v_t \,\sim\, \mathcal{N}(0,\, V),\; V \,=\, \sum_j V_j}. \]
\emph{Why one combined $v_t$?} The component noises $v_{j,t}$ are independent Gaussians, so their sum is Gaussian with variance $V = \sum_j V_j$. From the observed-side perspective, ``one obs noise per component'' and ``one combined obs noise $V$'' are \emph{indistinguishable} --- but the per-component split is useful for bookkeeping when each brick has its own well-understood noise scale (e.g., trend-irregular vs.\ measurement error).

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Combine --- 4-step DLM template.}}

\emph{(1) State (stack components).}
\[ \theta_t \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} \theta_{1,t} \\ \theta_{2,t} \\ \vdots \\ \theta_{k,t} \end{pmatrix} \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{p_1 + \cdots + p_k}. \]
each $\theta_{j,t}$ can be any dimension $p_j \geq 1$

\emph{(2) Component equations.} Already given above --- each $\theta_{j,t}$ evolves on its own with $G_j, W_j$.

\emph{(3) Matrix state equation (block-diagonal $G$ and $W$).}
\[ \theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t, \qquad w_t \overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,\, W_t), \qquad G = \mathrm{blkdiag}(G_1, \dots, G_k), \qquad W_t = \mathrm{blkdiag}(W_1, \dots, W_k). \]
The block-diagonal structure encodes ``no cross-coupling between components.''

\emph{(4) Observation equation (row-concatenate the $F$'s; sum the obs noises).}
\[ Y_t = F\, \theta_t + v_t, \qquad v_t \overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,\, V_t), \qquad F = [\,F_1 \;\big|\; F_2 \;\big|\; \cdots \;\big|\; F_k\,], \qquad V_t = \sum_{j=1}^k V_j. \]
\emph{Distributional form.} $\theta_t \mid \theta_{t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(G\theta_{t-1},\, W\bigr)$; $\;Y_t \mid \theta_t \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(F\theta_t,\, V\bigr)$ with $V = \sum_j V_j$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Why it works (intuition).}}

$\bullet$ \emph{Gaussian closure.} A sum of independent Gaussians is Gaussian. So $\sum_j F_j \theta_{j,t}$ is Gaussian, and adding $v_t$ keeps it Gaussian.

$\bullet$ \emph{Independence preserved by stacking.} Block-diagonal $G, W$ means the components NEVER mix in mean or in shocks --- they evolve in parallel. The Kalman recursion on the combined state effectively runs $k$ parallel recursions for the conditional means, with the \emph{only} cross-talk happening through the joint update against the observation $y_t$ (which sees the sum).

$\bullet$ \emph{Modularity.} You design each brick once (LLT, seasonal, AR, regression on $x_t$) and reuse them in any combination.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Canonical example --- Basic Structural Model (BSM).}}

\[ Y_t = \underbrace{\mu_t}_{\text{trend (LLT)}} + \underbrace{\gamma_t}_{\text{seasonal}} + \underbrace{v_t}_{\text{irregular}}. \]

\emph{Trend brick (LLT, $p_1 = 2$):} $\theta_{1,t} = (\mu_t, \beta_t)$, $G_1 = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $F_1 = (1,\, 0)$.

\emph{Quarterly seasonal brick ($p_2 = 3$):} $\theta_{2,t} = (\gamma_t,\, \gamma_{t-1},\, \gamma_{t-2})$, $G_2 = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $F_2 = (1, 0, 0)$.

\emph{Combined.} State $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^5$ (2 trend $+$ 3 seasonal), $G = \mathrm{blkdiag}(G_1, G_2)$, $F = [\,1\;0\;1\;0\;0\,]$. One DLM; one Kalman filter pass; outputs both the trend $\mu_t$ and the seasonal $\gamma_t$ separately as components of the filtered state.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{More compositions (same recipe).}}

$\bullet$ \emph{LLT + AR(1) cycle:} trend $+$ a stationary AR(1) for short-run fluctuations. State $\in \mathbb{R}^3$.

$\bullet$ \emph{LLT + regression on $x_t$:} trend $+$ DLR with time-varying $(\alpha_t, \beta_t)$. State $\in \mathbb{R}^4$. ``Trend plus covariate response.''

$\bullet$ \emph{Trend + seasonal + regression:} the classic Holt-Winters in DLM clothing.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Worked examples --- applying rows 5a + 5c at horizon $h$ (symbolic).}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Example I --- scalar $G = 2$.}}

\emph{Setup.} $\theta_t = 2\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t,\quad w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, W)$;\quad $Y_t = \theta_t + \varepsilon_t,\quad \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, V)$.

\emph{(5a) State forecast at $T + h$.}
\[ a_{T+h} \;=\; G^h\, m_T \;=\; 2^h\, m_T, \qquad R_{T+h} \;=\; G^{2h}\, C_T + W \sum_{k=0}^{h-1} G^{2k} \;=\; 4^h\, C_T + W\, \tfrac{4^h - 1}{3}. \]
\[ \theta_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\Bigl(2^h\, m_T,\;\; 4^h\, C_T + W\, \tfrac{4^h - 1}{3}\Bigr). \]

\emph{Proof of the $R_{T+h}$ form (unroll the state $h$ steps).} From $\theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t$:
\[ \theta_{T+h} \;=\; G^h\, \theta_T \;+\; \sum_{j=0}^{h-1} G^j\, w_{T+h-j}. \]
The current state $\theta_T$ is multiplied by $G^h$; each future shock $w_{T+k}$ ($k = 1, \dots, h$) is carried forward $h - k$ further steps, contributing weight $G^{h-k} = G^j$ (with $j = h - k$). Taking variance conditional on $y_{1:T}$ (using independence of $\theta_T$ and the shocks, and independence of the shocks across $t$):
\[ R_{T+h} \;=\; \mathrm{Var}(\theta_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T}) \;=\; G^h\, C_T\, (G^h)^\top \;+\; \sum_{j=0}^{h-1} G^j\, W\, (G^j)^\top, \]
which in the scalar case with $G = 2$ becomes $4^h\, C_T + W \sum_{j=0}^{h-1} 4^j = 4^h C_T + W\, \tfrac{4^h-1}{3}$ (geometric series).

\emph{(5c) $Y_{T+h}$ predictive.}
\[ f_{T+h} \;=\; F\, a_{T+h} \;=\; 2^h\, m_T, \qquad Q_{T+h} \;=\; F^2\, R_{T+h} + V \;=\; 4^h\, C_T + W\, \tfrac{4^h - 1}{3} + V. \]
\[ Y_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\Bigl(2^h\, m_T,\;\; 4^h\, C_T + W\, \tfrac{4^h - 1}{3} + V\Bigr). \]

\emph{Reading.} Forecast variance \textbf{explodes geometrically} ($\sim 4^h$) because $|G| > 1$ amplifies state variance every step.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Example II --- $2 \times 2$ $G$ (local linear trend: state $=$ level $+$ slope).}}

\emph{Setup.} $\theta_t = (\mu_t,\, \beta_t)^\top$;\quad $G = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$;\quad $F = (1,\, 0)$;\quad $W = \mathrm{diag}(W_\mu,\, W_\beta)$;\quad $V$ scalar. Filtered: $m_T = (m_\mu,\, m_\beta)^\top$,\quad $C_T = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}C_{\mu\mu} & C_{\mu\beta} \\ C_{\mu\beta} & C_{\beta\beta}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$. Note $G^h = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & h \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$.

\emph{(5a) State forecast at $T + h$.}
\[ a_{T+h} \;=\; G^h\, m_T \;=\; \begin{pmatrix}1 & h \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}m_\mu \\ m_\beta\end{pmatrix} \;=\; \begin{pmatrix}m_\mu + h\, m_\beta \\ m_\beta\end{pmatrix}. \]
\[ R_{T+h} \;=\; G^h\, C_T\, (G^h)^\top + \sum_{k=0}^{h-1} G^k\, W\, (G^k)^\top. \]
\[ \theta_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}_2(a_{T+h},\, R_{T+h}). \]

\emph{(5c) $Y_{T+h}$ predictive.}
\[ f_{T+h} \;=\; F\, a_{T+h} \;=\; m_\mu + h\, m_\beta, \qquad Q_{T+h} \;=\; F\, R_{T+h}\, F^\top + V \;=\; [R_{T+h}]_{1,1} + V. \]
Notice how you care just about the variance of the first row.
\[ Y_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}\!\bigl(m_\mu + h\, m_\beta,\;\; [R_{T+h}]_{1,1} + V\bigr). \]



\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Fully worked numerical example --- local-level (RW + noise), Case B, $W > 0$.}}

\emph{Parameters.} $W = \sigma_w^2 = 0.156$,\quad $V = \sigma^2 = 1$,\quad $m_0 = 1$,\quad $C_0 = 4$,\quad $y_1 = 2$,\quad $y_2 = 2.5$. $G = F = 1$ throughout.

\emph{After each numeric value, we show how to obtain it directly via $E[\cdot]$ and $\mathrm{Var}(\cdot)$ on the model equations --- no formula memorisation needed.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{At $t = 0$ --- forecast $\theta_1$ and $Y_1$ (no data yet).}}

\emph{Forecast state $\theta_1$.}
\[ a_1 \;=\; G\, m_0 \;=\; 1,\qquad R_1 \;=\; G^2\, C_0 + W \;=\; 4 + 0.156 \;=\; 4.156. \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta_1 \mid y_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(1,\, 4.156). \]
\emph{Derivation via $E/\mathrm{Var}$.} Since $\theta_1 = \theta_0 + w_1$ with $w_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, W)$ independent of $\theta_0$:
\[ a_1 = E[\theta_1] = E[\theta_0] + E[w_1] = m_0 + 0 = 1; \qquad R_1 = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_1) = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_0) + \mathrm{Var}(w_1) = C_0 + W = 4.156. \]

\emph{Forecast observation $Y_1$.}
\[ f_1 \;=\; F\, a_1 \;=\; 1, \qquad Q_1 \;=\; F^2\, R_1 + V \;=\; 4.156 + 1 \;=\; 5.156. \quad\Rightarrow\quad Y_1 \mid y_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(1,\, 5.156). \]
\emph{Derivation.} Since $Y_1 = \theta_1 + \varepsilon_1$ with $\varepsilon_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(0, V)$ independent:
\[ f_1 = E[Y_1] = E[\theta_1] + E[\varepsilon_1] = a_1 = 1; \qquad Q_1 = \mathrm{Var}(Y_1) = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_1) + \mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon_1) = R_1 + V = 5.156. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{At $t = 1$ --- observe $y_1 = 2$; filter $\theta_1$; forecast $\theta_2$ and $Y_2$.}}

\emph{Filter $\theta_1$.} Innovation $e_1 = y_1 - f_1 = 2 - 1 = 1$; gain $K_1 = R_1/(R_1 + V) = 4.156/5.156 \approx 0.806$.
\[ m_1 \;=\; a_1 + K_1\, e_1 \;=\; 1 + 0.806 \cdot 1 \;=\; 1.806, \qquad C_1 \;=\; (1 - K_1)\, R_1 \;\approx\; 0.806. \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta_1 \mid y_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(1.806,\, 0.806). \]
\emph{Derivation via $E/\mathrm{Var}$ (Bayes for jointly Gaussian $(\theta_1, Y_1)$).} The joint is $(\theta_1, Y_1) \sim \mathcal{N}\!\bigl((a_1, f_1),\; (\begin{smallmatrix}R_1 & R_1 \\ R_1 & Q_1\end{smallmatrix})\bigr)$

\[ m_1 = E[\theta_1 \mid Y_1 = y_1] = a_1 + R_1 Q_1^{-1}(y_1 - f_1) = 1 + 4.156/5.156 \cdot 1 = 1.806; \]
\[ C_1 = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_1 \mid Y_1 = y_1) = R_1 - R_1 Q_1^{-1} R_1 = 4.156 - 4.156^2/5.156 \approx 0.806. \]

\textbf{The crucial covariance entry to compute:}
\[ \boxed{\; \mathrm{Cov}(\theta_t, Y_t \mid y_{1:t-1}) \;=\; \mathrm{Cov}(\theta_t,\, F_t \theta_t + v_t \mid y_{1:t-1}) \;=\; F_t\, \mathrm{Var}(\theta_t \mid y_{1:t-1}) \;=\; F_t\, R_t \quad (\text{matrix form: } R_t F_t^\top). \;} \]



\emph{Forecast $\theta_2$.}
\[ a_2 \;=\; G\, m_1 \;=\; 1.806, \qquad R_2 \;=\; C_1 + W \;=\; 0.806 + 0.156 \;=\; 0.962. \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta_2 \mid y_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(1.806,\, 0.962). \]
\emph{Derivation.} $\theta_2 = \theta_1 + w_2$, $w_2 \perp \theta_1$ given $y_1$: $E[\theta_2 \mid y_1] = E[\theta_1 \mid y_1] = m_1$; $\mathrm{Var}(\theta_2 \mid y_1) = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_1 \mid y_1) + \mathrm{Var}(w_2) = C_1 + W$.

\emph{Forecast $Y_2$.}
\[ f_2 = F\, a_2 = 1.806, \qquad Q_2 = R_2 + V = 1.962. \quad\Rightarrow\quad Y_2 \mid y_1 \sim \mathcal{N}(1.806,\, 1.962). \]
\emph{Derivation.} $Y_2 = \theta_2 + \varepsilon_2$, $\varepsilon_2 \perp \theta_2$: $E[Y_2 \mid y_1] = E[\theta_2 \mid y_1] = a_2$; $\mathrm{Var}(Y_2 \mid y_1) = \mathrm{Var}(\theta_2 \mid y_1) + \mathrm{Var}(\varepsilon_2) = R_2 + V$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{At $t = 2$ --- observe $y_2 = 2.5$; filter $\theta_2$; forecast $\theta_3$ and $Y_3$.}}

\emph{Filter $\theta_2$.} $e_2 = y_2 - f_2 = 2.5 - 1.806 = 0.694$; $K_2 = R_2/(R_2+V) = 0.962/1.962 \approx 0.490$.
\[ m_2 \;=\; a_2 + K_2\, e_2 \;\approx\; 2.146, \qquad C_2 \;=\; (1 - K_2)\, R_2 \;\approx\; 0.491. \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta_2 \mid y_{1:2} \sim \mathcal{N}(2.146,\, 0.491). \]
\emph{Derivation.} Same Gaussian-conditioning argument as $t=1$, now with prior $\mathcal{N}(a_2, R_2)$ instead of $\mathcal{N}(a_1, R_1)$ and observation $y_2$:
\[ m_2 = a_2 + R_2 Q_2^{-1}(y_2 - f_2) = 1.806 + 0.962/1.962 \cdot 0.694 \approx 2.146; \]
\[ C_2 = R_2 - R_2 Q_2^{-1} R_2 = 0.962 - 0.962^2/1.962 \approx 0.491. \]

\emph{Forecast $\theta_3$.} $a_3 = m_2 = 2.146$; $R_3 = C_2 + W = 0.647$. \;\emph{Derivation:} $\theta_3 = \theta_2 + w_3 \Rightarrow E[\theta_3 \mid y_{1:2}] = m_2$, $\mathrm{Var}(\theta_3 \mid y_{1:2}) = C_2 + W$.

\emph{Forecast $Y_3$.} $f_3 = a_3 = 2.146$; $Q_3 = R_3 + V = 1.647$. \;\emph{Derivation:} $Y_3 = \theta_3 + \varepsilon_3 \Rightarrow E[Y_3 \mid y_{1:2}] = a_3$, $\mathrm{Var}(Y_3 \mid y_{1:2}) = R_3 + V$.


Adding them is the Bayesian honesty about uncertainty: forecast bands include both. The frequentist plug-in $Y_{T+1} \mid \hat\theta \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat\theta, \sigma^2)$ keeps only the $\sigma^2$ term and produces bands that are too narrow. IT IS SO OBVIOUS THE FREQUENTIST IS WRONG, JUT BECAUSE BEING $\hat \theta$ ESTIMATED it cannot be used as a fixed point. Note frequentists fixed this by a correction for estiamtion uncertainty

In Case b 5c, there is extra uncertainty by the fact the hidden state wonders freely. notice also in a random walk the random walk can end up being +1 million or -2 millions.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Example application --- random walk $+$ noise (full forecast chain).}}

\emph{Model.} State $\theta_t = \theta_{t-1} + w_t$, $w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, W)$. Obs $Y_t = \theta_t + v_t$, $v_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, V)$. So $G = 1, F = 1$.

\emph{Filter (state posterior at time $t$).} Given $\theta_{t-1} \mid y_{1:t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(m_{t-1},\, C_{t-1})$:
\[ \theta_t \mid y_{1:t} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}(m_t,\, C_t), \qquad K_t = \frac{C_{t-1} + W}{C_{t-1} + W + V}, \]
\[ m_t = m_{t-1} + K_t\,(y_t - m_{t-1}), \qquad C_t = (1 - K_t)(C_{t-1} + W). \]

\emph{1-step state forecast.}
\[ \theta_{t+1} \mid y_{1:t} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}(a_{t+1},\, R_{t+1}) \;=\; \mathcal{N}(m_t,\, C_t + W). \]
($G=1 \Rightarrow a_{t+1} = m_t$; one extra state shock $\Rightarrow$ variance bumped by $W$.)

\emph{$h$-step state forecast (iterate $h$ times --- mean stays put, variance accumulates $h$ shocks).}
\[ \theta_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}(\underbrace{m_T}_{a_{T+h}},\; \underbrace{C_T + h\, W}_{R_{T+h}}). \]

\emph{$h$-step observation forecast.}
\[ Y_{T+h} \mid y_{1:T} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}(\underbrace{m_T}_{f_{T+h}},\; \underbrace{C_T + h\, W + V}_{Q_{T+h}}). \]

\emph{Reading the variances.} State-forecast variance grows as $C_T + hW$ \emph{linearly in horizon} (one new state shock per step). Observation-forecast variance adds one more $V$ on top --- the fixed obs-noise contribution.

\emph{Numerical illustration (simulated, $T=40, W=0.4, V=1$).} $m_T \approx -3.33$, $C_T \approx 0.46$. State forecast variance at $h = 1, 10, 25$: $0.86,\, 4.46,\, 10.46$. Obs forecast variance: $1.86,\, 5.46,\, 11.46$.

